# VOIID — serving the app-association files for community invite links.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR
# ----------------
# Invite links are https://voiid.app/c/<handle>?i=<token> (docs/research/04_communities_plan.md
# §"Invite links"). Both platforms claim them as VERIFIED links — Android App Links, iOS
# Universal Links — and both verifications are a fetch of a file from the domain itself:
#
#   https://voiid.app/.well-known/assetlinks.json                 (Android)
#   https://voiid.app/.well-known/apple-app-site-association      (iOS)
#
# Until these are served, BOTH platforms fall back to the browser. That is a degraded feature,
# not a broken one, and it is why neither app ships a `voiid://` custom-scheme fallback: a
# custom scheme is registrable by any app on the device, and the OS would hand a live invite
# token to whichever one claimed it. A domain we control cannot be claimed by anyone else.
#
# BEFORE THIS WORKS, TWO THINGS ARE STILL MISSING:
#
#   1. assetlinks.json carries PLACEHOLDER fingerprints. Replace them with the real SHA-256
#      certificate digests:
#        - the Play App Signing key   (Play Console -> Setup -> App integrity), which is what
#          actually signs installs from the store, and
#        - the upload key and/or the local debug key, so verification also passes on internal
#          and developer builds.
#      Multiple fingerprints in the array is the supported way to cover all of them. Wrong or
#      missing fingerprints fail SILENTLY: links just open Chrome, with no error anywhere.
#      Verify with `adb shell pm verify-app-links --re-verify com.voiid.app` then
#      `adb shell pm get-app-links com.voiid.app`.
#
#   2. The apex A/AAAA record for voiid.app (and www) has to point at whatever box runs this
#      config. Today only api-dev.voiid.app is served (see ../Caddyfile.dev); this file is a
#      separate site block precisely so the API host and the public apex stay independent.
#
# NOTES THAT MATTER
# -----------------
# - The AASA file has NO .json extension and must be served as application/json. Caddy guesses
#   Content-Type from the extension, so it is set explicitly below.
# - Neither file may be behind a redirect. iOS follows none at all when fetching the AASA, and
#   an http->https redirect on the /.well-known path is the classic silent failure.
# - Both files are PUBLIC by design and contain no secrets: a bundle id, a package name and
#   certificate fingerprints, all of which are already readable from any installed copy of the
#   app. Nothing here touches E2EE material.
# - The www host must serve the same files. Android matches intent-filter hosts literally and
#   iOS looks up the AASA per-domain, so a link copied out of a desktop browser (routinely the
#   www form) falls through to the browser unless www is covered too.

voiid.app, www.voiid.app {
	# The association files. `header` before `file_server` so the Content-Type applies to
	# the extension-less AASA file as well.
	handle /.well-known/* {
		header Content-Type application/json
		root * /var/www/voiid.app
		file_server
	}

	# Everything else is the marketing site / whatever lands here later. Deliberately not
	# defined by this file: the app claims only /c/*, so the rest of the domain is free.

	encode gzip
}
